    <!-- Frequently Asked Questions Accordions -->
    <div class="space-y-3 pt-6 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            1. How do I calculate exponential growth?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          To calculate exponential growth, divide the percentage growth rate by 100, add 1 to obtain the multiplier, raise that multiplier to the power of the number of elapsed periods, and multiply by the starting quantity: x(t) = x₀ × (1 + r/100)ᵗ.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            2. What is the difference between exponential growth and linear growth?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Linear growth adds a constant numerical quantity in every period (e.g. +5 every year), creating a straight-line plot. Exponential growth multiplies the current quantity by a constant factor in every period, creating a curve that steepens progressively.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            3. How do I calculate exponential decay?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Exponential decay uses the exact same formula, but with a negative percentage rate r between 0% and -100%. The resulting multiplier (1 + r/100) is a decimal fraction between 0 and 1, causing the quantity to diminish over successive periods.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            4. What happens if the growth rate is -100%?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          A rate of -100% makes the base multiplier 1 + (-100/100) = 0. The quantity is completely annihilated to zero after the very first period. Rates below -100% are physically meaningless in standard quantity models because they produce negative multipliers.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            5. Can time (t) be negative?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Yes. A negative value of t indicates looking backward into the past relative to the baseline starting time t = 0. For example, t = -3 calculates what the quantity was 3 periods before the initial observation.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            6. What is the continuous exponential growth formula?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          The continuous formula is x(t) = x₀ × e^(kt), where e is Euler's constant (~2.71828) and k is the continuous rate constant, related to the periodic rate r by k = ln(1 + r/100).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            7. How do I calculate doubling time or half-life?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Doubling time for growth is calculated as t_double = ln(2) / ln(1 + r/100). For decay, the half-life is t_half = ln(0.5) / ln(1 + r/100).
        </p>
      </details>
